# `hidden` is a `const` that holds the one symbol made for it, for good. That is a constant and no memory: an access under it goes by the symbol as by a name, however the unit is walked. Bun's library leans on
# it: what it registers to run "before somebody looks under the symbol with a key that is a value" must not run at the library's own accesses. A slow version and a unit walked closed believed nothing of any
# variable: they took this one for any value, the access went by a key that is a value, and the library's own reads looked foreign. Now they ask Link's key at the access, as a fast version does, and take it where
# the variable bears it out. So Link's own rules hold here too: a symbol of the library is a name in the library's text alone, and symbols with a preparation are names all together or not at all.
# f1 = read, f2 = store
# -- source: an-access-under-a-variable-that-holds-one-symbol-for-good-goes-by-that-symbol-however-the-unit-is-walked.ts
